# Pre-Registration: Falsifiable Law-of-Form Test

Date: 2026-08-11
Test ID: LoF-001 (sonic domain, surrogate null model)

## Status
WRITTEN BEFORE data is downloaded or analyzed. Any edits after data
acquisition invalidate the test.

## Hypothesis (the claim under test)
"Coherent geometry will show stable fractal dimension ... persistent
recursion and coherence." Applied to audio: a real sonic signal exhibits
genuine long-range coherence (resonance persistence) that cannot be
explained by its trivial short-range statistical properties.

## Operational metric
For a 10-second excerpt of the signal:

1. Compute the power spectrum P(f) via FFT (Welch's method).
2. Fit a power-law exponent b to P(f) ~ f^b over the band 30 Hz - 8 kHz.
3. Compute the Hurst exponent H of the waveform.

A steeper, more negative spectral slope and H > 0.5 indicate persistence.

## Null model (the skeptic's position)
The measured spectrum and Hurst exponent arise purely from the signal's
stationary statistics. Create 200 phase-randomized surrogates: preserve
the exact power spectrum |FFT| but randomize the Fourier phases. This
destroys any true long-range coherence while keeping the spectrum.

## Pre-registered predictions

- P1 (SIGNAL vs SURROGATES): The real signal's Hurst exponent H_real
  will exceed the 99th percentile of the surrogate H distribution.
  If it does NOT, the Law-of-Form claim FAILS for this signal.

- P2 (STABILITY / scale invariance): The spectral slope b computed on
  three independent 3.3 s sub-windows will have a spread < 0.5.
  If spread >= 0.5, the "stable across scales" claim FAILS.

## Decision rules (fixed before seeing data)
- PASS requires BOTH P1 and P2.
- FAIL if either P1 or P2 fails.

## Honesty clause (read after running)
A PASS on a whale song would only demonstrate that a vocalizing animal
produces a coherent, self-similar signal — consistent with known
acoustics/biology. It does NOT establish a new physical law, because the
null model already incorporates the dominant physical explanation
(spectral shape). The test's value is bounded: it verifies the metric is
sensitive and the effect is real, not imagined.
